//# #2737 / #2778: interpolating a formal whose spelling an ENCLOSING binder
//# also bound is refused.
//
// #2778 threads a nested binder's own bound: `rewrite_expr`'s EFn arm overlays
// the lambda's dictionaries on the enclosing ones and drops the rows it
// rebinds, so the other five rungs of this family -- qualified, UFCS, the
// method as a value, the kinded binder, and an inner binder spelled apart from
// the outer one -- now dispatch correctly and their fixtures are gone. This one
// is what is LEFT, and it is left for a reason of its own.
//
// `dtd_show_witness_dict` declines a formal that `dtd_formal_is_shadowed`
// reports, and here the inner `[T: Show]` shadows an outer `[T: Show]` of the
// same spelling. Under the overlay that guard is arguably redundant -- the
// outer row is dropped, so the only dictionary reachable for `T` is the inner
// one, and `inner(Qt::{ w: 5 })` would render `Qt!`. Arguably is not measured,
// and the pre-#2778 measurement of this exact program is on record: `outer` at
// `Pt` with `inner` applied to a `Qt` printed `Pt!` -- a `Qt` rendered through
// `Pt::to_string` -- and with the dictionary withheld instead it printed `284`,
// the tagged pointer. `Pt!` is a lie about the type and `284` is not
// information.
//
// So the shadowed spelling stays refused until someone measures it, which is
// the fail-closed side of "never be silently wrong". Removing the guard is the
// remaining half of #2778, and the green side for every UNSHADOWED nested rung
// is `fixtures/lambda_bound_nested_witness_test.vibe`.
//
// A SCALAR does render truthfully, and it never reaches here: `interp_shape`
// answers first. That is also why a top-level `[T: Show]` applied to `7` prints
// `7` and not `Int!`.

trait Show {
  to_string(Self) -> String
}

struct Pt {
  v: Int
}

struct Qt {
  w: Int
}

impl Show for Pt {
  to_string(a: Pt) -> String {
    "Pt!"
  }
}

impl Show for Qt {
  to_string(a: Qt) -> String {
    "Qt!"
  }
}

fn outer_show[T: Show](x: T) -> String {
  let inner = [T: Show](a: T) -> String {
    "\{a}"
  }
  inner(Qt::{ w: 5 })
}

export fn _start() -> Int {
  String::length(outer_show(Pt::{ v: 1 }))
}
